county's professional football teams as well as for the National Team, England. Hancock began his career as a junior with Plymouth Argyle, turning professional in September 1955. He played twice in the league for Argyle during the 1956–57 season, but failed to make any further appearances and left to join Torquay United in January 1959. He made 177 league appearances for Torquay, scoring twelve goals, and played in the\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0301-40fbda82e379:d01\", \"text\": \"David Hancock (cricketer) David Arthur Hancock (born 28 March 1940) is a former English cricketer. Hancock was a left-handed batsman. He was born in Newcastle-under-Lyme, Staffordshire. Hancock made his debut for Staffordshire in the 1958 Minor Counties Championship against the Yorkshire Second XI. Hancock played Minor counties cricket for Staffordshire from 1958 to 1983, which included 204 Minor Counties Championship matches. In his entire Minor Counties Championship career for Staffordshire, he scored 9,303 runs at an average of 27.93, making 51 half centuries and 8 centuries, which included a high score of 148. He made his List A debut for\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0301-40fbda82e379:d02\", \"text\": \"retirement after serving one term in the Legislature for the Alberta Liberal Party left open the constituency of Edmonton-Whitemud, the riding once held by former Premier Don Getty. Hancock secured the PC nomination and squared off against Corky Meyer of the Liberals and Charan Khehra of the Alberta New Democratic Party in the 1997 provincial election. He won the constituency with 51 per cent of the vote, beating runner-up Meyer by 2,020 votes and was one of only two Progressive Conservatives elected in Edmonton's 19 constituencies (Julius Yankowsky, Edmonton-Beverly-Clareview, was the other.) Hancock was returned to the Legislature in the\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [\"Cricket\"], \"prior_explanation\": \"David Hancock is a former English cricketer. I am certain based on general sports knowledge, as he played first-class cricket for Derbyshire.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0301-40fbda82e379:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0301-40fbda82e379:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"David Hancock\", \"scope\": \"sport association\", \"claim\": \"David Jeffrey Hancock was an English professional footballer.\", \"answer\": \"football\", \"quote\": \"Dave Hancock (footballer) David Jeffrey Hancock (24 July 1938 – 6 June 2007) was an English professional footballer.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0301-40fbda82e379:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0301-40fbda82e379:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"David Hancock\", \"scope\": \"sport association\", \"claim\": \"David Arthur Hancock is a former English cricketer.\", \"answer\": \"cricket\", \"quote\": \"David Hancock (cricketer) David Arthur Hancock (born 28 March 1940) is a former English cricketer.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}], \"fallible_previous_answer\": [\"football\", \"cricket\"]}"
